{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1910,
    "dDurationMs": 5069,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Only a few elements can be permanent magnets\n- iron is one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6979,
    "dDurationMs": 1921,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Copper is not."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8900,
    "dDurationMs": 8580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if you pass an electric current through\nany metal it becomes a magnet - an electromagnet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17480,
    "dDurationMs": 1260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But how does this work?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18740,
    "dDurationMs": 4609,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well strangely enough, it's a consequence\nof special relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23349,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Special relativity is the fact that in our\nuniverse, length and time aren't absolute;"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28289,
    "dDurationMs": 4761,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they're perceived differently by observers\nmoving relative to each other (hence, \"relativity\")."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33050,
    "dDurationMs": 4430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, if you measure carefully enough,\nyou'll find that time passes slower for observers"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37480,
    "dDurationMs": 1730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "moving relative to you."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39210,
    "dDurationMs": 2110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey Derek, when did you last shave?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41320,
    "dDurationMs": 1500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Derek1: Six hours ago."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42820,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "MovingDerek: Actually it was five hours, 59\nminutes and 59.99999999999 seconds"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49620,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And moving objects are also contracted in\ntheir direction of motion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53180,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You're looking slim."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54180,
    "dDurationMs": 2129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Only in your frame of reference."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56309,
    "dDurationMs": 4151,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So when an object is moving relative to you,\nit actually takes up less space than when"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60460,
    "dDurationMs": 1070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's not moving."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61530,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And even though this effect is obviously way\ntinier than we've shown, length contraction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65630,
    "dDurationMs": 2980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "IS what makes an electromagnet work."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68610,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Picture a copper wire - it consists of positive\nmetal ions swimming in a sea of free negative"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74370,
    "dDurationMs": 1140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75510,
    "dDurationMs": 3940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now the number of protons is equal to the\nnumber of negative electrons so overall the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79450,
    "dDurationMs": 1360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wire is neutral."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80810,
    "dDurationMs": 4550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if there were a positive charged, err...\npositively charged cat nearby, it would experience"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85360,
    "dDurationMs": 2250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no force from the wire on it at all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87610,
    "dDurationMs": 3740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And even if there were a current in the wire,\nthe electrons would just be drifting in one"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91350,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "direction, but the density of positive and\nnegative charges would still be the same,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95150,
    "dDurationMs": 3500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so the wire would be neutral, so no force\non the kitty."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98650,
    "dDurationMs": 2930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what if the cat starts moving?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101580,
    "dDurationMs": 4950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Imagine for simplicity that that the cat is\nmoving in the same direction as the electrons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106530,
    "dDurationMs": 1760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with the same velocity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108290,
    "dDurationMs": 3380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well now in my frame of reference, the wire\nis still neutral and so there should be no"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111670,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "force on the cat, but consider the same situation\nin her frame of reference."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116650,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the cat's frame of reference the positive\ncharges in the wire are moving, so according"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121150,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to special relativity their separation will\nbe ever-so-slightly contracted."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124790,
    "dDurationMs": 3889,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Also, from this perspective the electrons\naren't moving so they'll be more spread out"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128679,
    "dDurationMs": 4261,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than before - remember, objects take up more\nspace when they're not moving than when they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132940,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133940,
    "dDurationMs": 3140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These two changes together mean there's a\nhigher density of positive charges in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137080,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wire, so it's no longer neutral - it's positively\ncharged!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140500,
    "dDurationMs": 3970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which means that the positively charged cat\nwill feel a repulsive electric force from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144470,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the wire."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145470,
    "dDurationMs": 5410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in my frame of reference this seems mysterious:\nthere's no force on a stationary charged cat,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150880,
    "dDurationMs": 4790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but a moving cat is somehow repelled from\nthis neutral wire."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155670,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How do you account for this force?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 157890,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well we say it is the magnetic force, and\nthat's mainly because a wire with current"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162550,
    "dDurationMs": 2020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in it deflects nearby magnets."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164570,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So really, what this experiment shows is that\na magnetic field is just an electric field"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168810,
    "dDurationMs": 2330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "viewed from a different frame of reference."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171140,
    "dDurationMs": 3940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the cat's frame of reference, it is repelled\nfrom the wire due to the electric field created"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175080,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by the excess positive charges produced by\nthe effects of length contraction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179280,
    "dDurationMs": 3710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In MY frame of reference, the cat is repelled\nfrom a neutral wire due to the magnetic field"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182990,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "generated by current flowing in the wire."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185790,
    "dDurationMs": 3410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So whether you see it as an electric or a\nmagnetic field just depends on your frame"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 189200,
    "dDurationMs": 3340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of reference, but in either case the results\nare the same."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192540,
    "dDurationMs": 5279,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So an electromagnet is an everyday example\nof special relativity in action."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197819,
    "dDurationMs": 4291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now that might seem crazy since electrons\ndrift through wires at about .0000000001%"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202110,
    "dDurationMs": 8820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the speed of light - so how can special relativity\nhave anything to do with it?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 210930,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well the truth is there are enough electrons\nin a wire, and the electric interaction is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215400,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so amazingly strong that even the minuscule\neffects of length contraction can produce"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220700,
    "dDurationMs": 3870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "significant charge imbalances that produce\na noticeable force."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224570,
    "dDurationMs": 4490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So special relativity explains electromagnets\n- but what about permanent magnets?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229060,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yeah!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230060,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mean there can't be electrical currents\nflowing around inside lumps of rock, can there?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234340,
    "dDurationMs": 4170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Click here to go to MinutePhysics where we'll\nexplore magnetite, compasses and all the wizardry"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238510,
    "dDurationMs": 2180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of permanent magnets."
    } ]
  } ]
}
